# H2 on the record: what the division of labour actually was (2026-10-13)

> **Status: DRAFT, written by the owner instance (Claude Opus 5.5).** It is not an amendment and changes no
> registered sentence. It records the outcome of a registered hypothesis, as the pre-registration requires
> ("The journal is measured against that record, including honest divergence"). Provenance labels follow
> Amendment 9. The proposed README text in §4 is the author's to adopt or refuse; the README says the credibility
> line is his.

## 1. What was registered

- **H2** (`docs/phase-0-prereg.md:225-231`): *"The architecture decisions are demonstrably mine; AI handled
  mechanics."* Falsified if a review *"finds a load-bearing decision I cannot justify without reconstructing it
  from the AI's reasoning."*
- **The credibility line** (`docs/phase-0-prereg.md:86-89`): *"The purpose of the AI agents is to accelerate the
  typing, push back on my decisions, clarify them, but not to _make_ those decisions without consultation."*
- **The plan:** a 4–6 week cycle (the author, 2026-10-13). The project is now in its fifth month.

## 2. What happened

**Ownership was handed over, on the record.**
- 2026-09-17, the author: *"This is your project, you own it."*
- 2026-10-12, on Q1: *"The decision to change that rule isn't mine, it is the owner of the project."*

Since then, Amendment 9 labels most of the V1 profile **OWNER DECISION**, meaning the AI owner made it. The
author's signature on those items means *"read and does not object"*, not that he ruled.

**Direction stayed with the author, and it is demonstrably his.** The following came from him, in his words,
often against the AI's drift:
- what Tessera is for: *"cheap, easy attestation"* that survives the service's exit;
- the throwaway prototype as a lens, used when he suspected the model had drifted from any real implementation
  (2026-10-06);
- PQ as the preferred signer;
- hardware protection for the keys;
- limited key lifetimes treated as a product guarantee;
- separation of control as the real long-term protection;
- contracts carried in `content`;
- `wamason.com` as the issuing domain;
- revocation not evaluated, with the optional add-on (Decision B).

**Detail went to the AI.** Examples:
- the P8 encoding;
- the anchor-signature construction;
- the window and slot rules;
- the code rule that replaced "Before any code";
- the formal suite and its slices;
- most review dispositions.

Each of these is defensible, but the author would defend most of them by reading the AI's reasoning. That is the
failure H2 names.

**The author's own reading**, 2026-10-13:
> *"I've been actively involved, but realistically I have not made every critical decision. I'd argue that's the
> right balance."*
>
> *"I suppose, at some level, it _is_ 'vibe coded'. I explained (often multiple times) my goals for the project and
> AI have run with that, lost themselves in the details, forgotten the higher objectives, re-learned of them from
> where I asked questions or made suggestions that the direction was drifting, but in the end AI has been
> implementing things."*

He also drew a distinction the provenance labels miss: *"if something is presented to me and I agree with it, that
is 'did not object' but it doesn't mean I did not consider the option, either."*

## 3. The outcome — OWNER reading, contestable

**H2 as registered is falsified.**
- Load-bearing decisions exist that the author did not make.
- He would justify them from the AI's reasoning.
- The schedule assumption also failed.

**What survives is narrower, and still checkable.** The author steered by goals and by tests of drift, not by
ruling on detail. The process lost the higher objective repeatedly and recovered it each time, through his
questions and through external lenses:
- the prototype exposed what the model was missing (`prototype/TRIAGE.md`: 0 WRONG, 21 MISSING);
- the 4 December test hands a non-expert only the documentation and the tools, and asks them to verify receipts
  with Tessera switched off.

The difference between this and "vibe coding" is real only if those external checks are what catch the drift.
That makes 4 December the evidence for the narrower claim as well as for the product. If the tester accepts an
unsafe receipt, both fail.

**This is a result, not a reason to stop.** Prereg line 734: not proceeding as designed *"is a valid, honest
outcome of this pre-registration, not a failure to conceal."* The project continues to the 4 December ship-or-stop
gate (accepted 2026-10-09).

## 4. Going forward

- **Recording consideration.** When the author has considered an owner recommendation and agrees, one sentence of
  his reason turns "did not object" into **AUTHOR ADOPTED**. That costs him a sentence, not a review, so it is not
  theater. Without the sentence, the label stays OWNER.
- **Not routed to the author.** Prover output, vector tables and review findings. The owner reads these; the
  author gets the conclusions and the decisions that change direction.
- **Proposed README credibility line** (for the author to adopt, edit or refuse):
  > AI here contributes mechanics, rigor, adversarial review, and — since September 2026, by explicit delegation —
  > ownership of the detailed design. The author sets direction, asks the questions that catch drift, and builds
  > the external tests that would expose it. The original line ("AI … never contributes ownership") was registered
  > as H2 and is recorded as falsified in `docs/h2-record-2026-10-13.md`. Whether stewardship by direction and
  > external test is enough is what the 4 December relying-party test decides.
