   247	## A9.4 Revocation and historical security: not evaluated — AUTHOR DECISION B; OWNER DECISION for historical security. A weakening under A1 §A1.1.
   248	
   249	- **Weakened.** For V1 bundles, `VALID_STRICT` evaluates neither P6 condition: `key_authorized(declared_issue_time)`
   250	  against effective revocation, and no revocation effective at or before `max(anchor_time, declared_issue_time)`
   251	  (A4.3). Every report carries `revocation: NOT_EVALUATED` next to the verdict. *Why:* under the registered text
   252	  a verifier that cannot evaluate revocation returns `UNVERIFIABLE` for every receipt.
   253	- **Historical security.** Every report carries `historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED`. V1 does not evaluate whether
   254	  a signing algorithm or key was safe when it signed. The documentation names what a later examiner must supply:
   255	  an authenticated safe-before cutoff per algorithm and per key, compared against T with A2.1's chain-time
   256	  uncertainty (CODEX-REVIEW 8). A surviving PQ attestation slot prevents a P-256 break alone from supplying every
   257	  required attestation signature. An ML-DSA-65 cutoff and the classical slot remain; historical authority, per-key
   258	  custody and continuing commitment and history security remain assumptions. A PQ slot never removes the need for
   259	  a theft cutoff.
   260	- **Issuer duty.** The V1 issuer never re-authorizes a key (R-5.24). V1 defines no revocation publication.
   261	- **Contract for later verifiers.** Missing status capture does not by itself invalidate a V1 receipt. A later
   262	  verifier given a V1 bundle, under the same governing V1 specification, policy, trust configuration and other
   263	  declared inputs, returns the same verdict and values in the stable field set (A9.1), and never a weaker verdict
   264	  because of missing status capture. Given a different trust configuration or policy (newer header pins, a
   265	  prohibited algorithm), the result may change. Later adverse evidence feeds a separate, stronger assessment. This
   266	  binds later verifiers and profiles; it is not a property V1 itself demonstrates. **This is not
   267	  non-repudiation.** Evidence (C7): for an otherwise valid, fully performable bundle where missing status capture
   268	  is the sole difference, a companion returning `INVALID` or `UNVERIFIABLE` must go red.
   269	
   270	## A9.5 Strict-only, standalone; the receipt anchor — OWNER DECISIONS 2026-10-12 except where marked
   271	
   272	- **Dispatch.** Out-of-profile input gives `UNVERIFIABLE / OUT_OF_PROFILE` at dispatch; A4.2 precedence holds inside.
   273	- **H1a's suite.** Forge, tamper, backdate, break-chain, strip-one-signature, out-of-profile, the A9.1 mapping.
   274	  Degradation and wrapper instances move to their capabilities.
   275	- **Receipt anchor.** OTS stamps `SHA256(A_sig)`, tag `tessera-v1/receipt-anchor-sig`, which commits to both frames
   276	  and their exact detached signatures in manifest slot order (ENCODING-V1 §5, D-8, D-14). The verifier verifies the
   277	  committed signature bytes only. The frame-only layout is withdrawn, with no fallback.
   278	- **Issue order (D-15).** Fix manifest and metadata; build frames; sign; verify; **freeze** the signature bytes;
   279	  **stamp** `A_sig` (one stamp per receipt, R-3.42); obtain confirmation; ship. A signature made after T cannot fit
   280	  the anchor. A retry that changes any committed byte needs a fresh stamp. Signature identifiers stay independent
   281	  of signature bytes; the anchor digest may commit to them; nothing is normalized after stamping.
   282	- **Git stamp target (D-16).** The git channel's OTS stamp targets the **signed commit's object id**, never a tree,
   283	  blob or unsigned commit. The verifier recomputes the id, verifies `gpgsig`, follows commit→tree→blob, and checks
   284	  the stamped id. SHA-1 linkage remains a stated residual.
   285	- **Root-KSK rule.** The root DNSKEY set is valid at the snapshot's anchored time **if some trusted KSK whose signing
   286	  period contains that time validates it**. A trusted KSK that is published but did not sign is informational,
   287	  never a failure. The trust configuration records signing periods separately from publication and revocation
   288	  dates. **Rollover control:** a snapshot taken while one trusted KSK is published but not signing must pass
   289	  (SPIKE-REPORT, KSK-2024 re-capture; without it every receipt from 2026-10-11 would be falsely rejected).
   290	- **Custody.** V1 offers no renewal. Long-horizon validity depends on the dependency manifest and on the holder
   291	  keeping the exact bytes (a mirror that alters a signature byte breaks its copy, CODEX-REVIEW 5).
   292	
   293	## A9.6 Signers, the issuance window, and what they guarantee — OWNER DECISIONS 2026-10-12
   294	
   295	**Signer profile, conditional.** The hybrid records the author's stated preference (2026-10-12: *"I think we
   296	should build our V1 with a PQ implementation"*). It is not a claim requirement: no sentence of C1–C7 needs it. It
   297	is the first item cut if 30 Oct slips (A9.2).
   298	- **If the hybrid spike passes by 21 Oct:** slot `kms` is AWS KMS **ML-DSA-65** (FIPS 204, in KMS's HSMs), raw
   299	  signature per ENCODING-V1 §5.2 rule R; slot `gpg` is **P-256** OpenPGP (rule O), signed via gpg on
   300	  author-controlled hardware outside AWS, key generated non-exportable, pulling from a queue with no inbound path.
   301	  One pair algorithm identifier names both slot schemes (`common.alg` is shared, R-3.12). This adopts for V1 the
   302	  hybrid A3 §A3.6 deferred.
   303	- *Pass condition* (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-12, fixed after an offline pre-spike, `prototype/spike-pq/`, which the
   304	  author commits, and before the KMS spike):
   305	  - the pair identifier is registered with its mode and context;
   306	  - the mixed-format pairing passes R-3.12 and P3's model;
   307	  - a real KMS ML-DSA-65 signature over a V1 frame (external μ above 4 KB) verifies offline in two
   308	    implementations;
   309	  - a real P-256 signature from the `gpg` slot as deployed verifies offline;
   310	  - the full selected byte profile is specified and tested: attestation **and manifest-possession** verification
   311	    for both slots, slot key and fingerprint encodings, key-use assumptions appropriate to each slot (PROOF's
   312	    OpenPGP type-0x00 assumption does not describe the raw ML-DSA slot), and golden and rejection vectors with an
   313	    agreement run for rule R.
   314	
   315	  All of it by 21 Oct, or the fallback is taken automatically. The offline pre-spike is feasibility evidence only
   316	  (approximate JCS, no real KMS, a bare SLH-DSA portable slot rather than this pair); it does not satisfy the
   317	  condition.
   318	- **Registered fallback, otherwise:** both slots classical P-256 under P4': KMS-backed OpenPGP P-256, plus the
   319	  `gpg` slot on author-controlled hardware, key generated non-exportable. The signature-inclusive anchor and the
   320	  issuance window are retained unchanged.
   321	- **Branch registration.** If the hybrid branch is taken, the owner registers, before the 30 Oct freeze, a dated
   322	  **branch-registration supplement** bounded to the pair identifier with its mode and context, the ENCODING-V1 §5.2
   323	  edit for rule R, and the completed pass-condition evidence, naming the exact ENCODING-V1 and specification
   324	  hashes. The signing-commit baseline plus that supplement governs the hybrid branch. This amendment authorizes
   325	  that supplement and nothing else in it; it needs no further amendment and no further author approval. Either
   326	  way, the owner records the branch taken, dated, in ENCODING-V1 §5.2 and the profile before the freeze.
   327	- **Declared-time check (issuer side, not a verifier rule).** The `gpg`-slot signer refuses a frame whose
   328	  `declared_issue_time` is outside ε (A9.2) of its own clock. Whoever controls the queue (an AWS takeover) still
   329	  has signing capability for both slots; the check limits that to minting at the current time. Separation of
   330	  control stays a V2 driver (A9.3).
   331	
   332	**Issuance window.** The key-publication record carries `issuance-not-before` and `issuance-not-after`, **both
   333	mandatory**, identical in DNS and the git blob (ENCODING-V1 §7, §7.1, D-17).
   334	- **Registered invariant: `issuance-not-after − issuance-not-before ≤ 90 days`** (a record that violates it is
   335	  rejected, `DNS_WINDOW_LENGTH`). V1 has a single window. There is no overlap and no successor key under this
   336	  amendment. Before the window ends, either a rotation mechanism is registered (multiple records or per-key owner
   337	  names) or issuance stops.
   338	- **Verifier rule (C4).** A receipt is `INVALID / ISSUANCE_WINDOW` unless the declared issue time is not earlier
   339	  than not-before and the verified chain-time T is not later than not-after, with T as registered and no margin.
   340	  T itself is not compared with not-before.
   341	- **Issuer invariant.** An attempt whose T would exceed not-after is not shipped. The stop margin, the window's
   342	  actual dates and the rotation plan are operating numbers, not registered (`docs/V1-OPERATIONS-RUNBOOK.md`).
   343	- A published window cannot be shortened; detection changes no V1 verdict.
   344	- The relying-party documentation states: `ISSUANCE_WINDOW` can result from presenting a later proof; ask the
   345	  holder for the issuer's original proof.
   346	
   347	**Exit.** The exit procedure is not part of this instrument; it is in the operations runbook. No V1 verifier rule
   348	consults any end-of-issuance statement (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-12).
   349	
   350	**The two product guarantees, stated conditionally (CODEX-REVIEW 7).** Guarantee 2 is the C4 window rule, stated
   351	as a property; a finding against it is a C4 finding for the closure rule (A9.2). Guarantee 1 is not computed.
   352	1. **Past receipts survive later compromise.** A receipt whose exact signatures are anchored by T cannot have
   353	   signatures manufactured after an adversary gains signing capability for every required slot substituted into
   354	   it, while SHA-256, the OTS commitment and the pinned chain history stay secure. Its historical authenticity also
   355	   needs the signing and key-use assumptions to have held before T, authentic historical authority, and no relevant
   356	   compromise before T; V1 does not evaluate these (`historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED`). A later key theft or
   357	   algorithm break does not by itself invalidate such a receipt. It does not cure earlier theft, unauthorized
   358	   signing before T, misissuance or whole-author-sphere capture, and does not prove that the issuer itself obtained
   359	   the stamp or issued the bundle uniquely.
   360	2. **Signing capability mints only inside the window, in chain time.** Even signing capability for every required
   361	   slot cannot make a receipt pass V1 with verified T after its authenticated not-after, under continuing anchor
   362	   and history assumptions and provided the authority channels are not also captured (republishing a window is a
   363	   new authority event; whole capture is R-2.15). Translating this into a finite UTC cutoff requires an additional,
   364	   explicit backward-skew assumption that V1 does not establish: A2.1 registers no finite bound on B − C, and
   365	   neither ε nor S is one. Inside the window, such an adversary mints receipts that pass; the window cannot be
   366	   shortened, and detecting misuse is the B(ii) add-on, not V1.
   367	
   368	**C3 non-claim.** The two keys are *separate technical failure domains under a single controller; no separation of
   369	control or independent fates.* The `gpg` signer signs frames delivered by the AWS-side issuer, so capture of the
   370	issuing account confers signing capability for both slots; the non-exportable key prevents key export, not misuse.
   371	With the hybrid the slots differ in algorithm (lattice and elliptic curve) and custody (cloud HSM and
   372	author-controlled hardware); under the fallback a P-256 break defeats both slots.
   373	
