   222	## 6. Findings that misread, or partly misread, the spec
   223	
   224	1. **Case (e)'s `VALID_STRICT` for a revoked key** (PHASE2 l.160, PHASE1 report "revocation
   225	   inside the window (`INVALID`)"). The prototype's verifier (`verifier.py:315-317`) reports `REV` as
   226	   `PASS` when no input exists. R-5.33 (l.1384) forbids reporting a required check as passed when it was
   227	   not performed. The report says so itself, but then counts the result as an unsafe accept the spec
   228	   produces. The spec gap (G-27) is real; the `VALID_STRICT` is the prototype's.
   229	2. **"No rule gets both c and h right"** (PHASE2 l.146-148; G-44). R-6.44 (l.1705) does route wrapped
   230	   standing to S-P7. But the author's 2026-09-15 ruling, formulation W (CAP l.704-709), reports standing
   231	   over a wrapped presentation, bound to the innermost issuer. The spec (dated 2026-09-13) does not carry
   232	   it. Under W, the careful rule accepts h on the innermost layer's standing and rejects c on its
   233	   `INVALID`. The finding is a stale-spec defect, not a model gap.
   234	3. **"The submitter's self-check is `UNVERIFIABLE` until it upgrades its verifier"** (PHASE2 l.83-90;
   235	   G-31, G-52). R-5.4 (l.1184), the §3.5 table and V37 (l.1347) let the headers travel in the bundle.
   236	   The failure comes from the prototype's rule that every header must equal a store pin; V38 is open
   237	   (O-22).
   238	4. **R-4.88(ii) versus R-2.2** (PHASE2 l.83). In context (l.1101, the SC-1 costs, where item (i) is the
   239	   *second* anchoring), "a bundle ships before confirmation" most plausibly refers to the TLR's pending
   240	   anchor, not the receipt's. R-2.2 is probably not contradicted. The author should confirm.
   241	5. **Design question 5, "should the bundle bind the store?"** (PHASE2 l.323). This half-misreads the
   242	   spec. R-5.8 deliberately ships the store with the verifier (P9), and V38 already names "store identity
   243	   pinned in declared verifier policy". The open question is O-22, not a new design gap.
   244	6. **K-1** is not a contradiction, as the prototype itself marks it ("apparent"). "Not validated" in
   245	   R-3.36 means no policy check. The equality pins are binding.
   246	
   247	## 7. Comparison with long-term-validation standards
   248	
   249	*Sources are the standards named. Recollections I could not verify against the text here are marked
   250	"(unverified)". Tessera's spec has no section that cites any of these.*
   251	
   252	- **RFC 3161 (Time-Stamp Protocol).**
   253	  - *What it does.* A trusted TSA signs a `TimeStampToken`, a CMS SignedData over a `messageImprint`
   254	    hash, carrying `genTime` and a policy OID. It carries no revocation data itself. The token's own
   255	    validity depends on the TSA certificate and its revocation status, checked separately.
   256	  - *Tessera.* Tessera replaces the TSA with OpenTimestamps/Bitcoin, which needs no trusted third party.
   257	    The price is that block time is miner-set within consensus bounds, hence δ/ε. Tessera has no
   258	    analogue of TSA-certificate validation; header authentication (V38) plays that role and is open.
   259	- **RFC 5126 CAdES, now ETSI EN 319 122-1/-2.**
   260	  - *What it does.* It defines levels: BES/EPES, then -T (a signature timestamp), -C (certificate and
   261	    revocation *references*), -X (timestamps over those references), -XL (the certificate and revocation
   262	    *values* embedded: `certificate-values`, `revocation-values`), and -A (a renewable
   263	    `archive-time-stamp`). EN 319 122 renames these baseline B-B / B-T / B-LT / B-LTA.
   264	  - *Revocation.* Status (CRL/OCSP) is collected after signing, ideally after a grace period (RFC 5126's
   265	    "grace period"; I could not confirm the clause number), and embedded so that validation needs no
   266	    live access.
   267	  - *Tessera.* Tessera has -T (the anchor) and the archive-renewal idea (wrapper renewal). It has no -LT
   268	    equivalent: nothing embeds revocation status (G-27).
   269	- **PAdES, ETSI EN 319 142.**
   270	  - *What it does.* The same levels for PDF. The LT material lives in the Document Security Store (DSS)
   271	    dictionary: certificates, CRLs and OCSP responses, with optional per-signature VRI entries. LTA adds
   272	    document timestamps. Status is added incrementally after signing, by any party.
   273	  - *Tessera.* The same gap as CAdES. PAdES's incremental augmentation is the model for how a holder
   274	    could add status after shipping. Tessera's only post-ship additions are the TLR and wrappers.
   275	- **ETSI EN 319 102-1 (signature validation procedures).**
   276	  - *What it does.* It separates basic validation, validation with time (the signature timestamp as
   277	    proof of existence, POE) and validation with long-term data. The last includes **past-signature
   278	    validation**, which checks the chain and revocation status *at the POE time* rather than now.
   279	  - *Outcomes.* It reports TOTAL-PASSED / TOTAL-FAILED / INDETERMINATE, with sub-indications
   280	    such as `REVOKED_NO_POE`, `TRY_LATER` and `OUT_OF_BOUNDS_NO_POE` (from memory; check the exact list
   281	    against the text).
   282	  - *Tessera.* The three verdicts map almost one to one onto VALID / INVALID / UNVERIFIABLE. Tessera's
   283	    interval rule (R-5.21, revocation compared against `anchor_time`) is a past-signature validation that
   284	    uses the anchor as the POE. What it lacks is the revocation *data* and a "try later" sub-indication
   285	    (P2-4).
   286	- **RFC 4998 ERS (ASN.1) and RFC 6283 XMLERS (XML).**
   287	  - *What they do.* An Evidence Record is a sequence of ArchiveTimeStampChains over a Merkle hash tree of
   288	    data objects. Timestamp renewal happens before the TSA certificate or its algorithm weakens. Hash-tree
   289	    renewal rehashes everything under a new hash algorithm. The material needed to validate each
   290	    timestamp (certificates, CRLs) is to be stored with it before renewal (RFC 4998 §5;
   291	    the exact placement is unverified).
   292	  - *Tessera.* This is the closest analogue to Tessera's custodial renewal (R-7.1 claim 3, P7 wrappers).
   293	    Tessera specifies no hash-tree renewal: everything rests on SHA-256 (PHASE2 l.262-263). It also has
   294	    no rule for storing validation data for its own anchors and evidences at renewal time.
   295	
   296	**What all five do that Tessera does not.** Each embeds the *status* evidence a later validator needs,
   297	collected from live sources at augmentation time by some party, and then protected by a later
   298	timestamp. Tessera forbids live and log sources at verification (R-5.10), but it never names an
   299	augmentation-time collector. That is the root of G-27.
   300	
   301	**Options for G-27, with what each requires of the models** (no recommendation):
   302	
   303	- **(A) The verifier does not enforce revocation; the adjudicator does** (R-1.22 already lets the
   304	  adjudicator use anything).
   305	  - *P6 / R-5.21.* `VALID_STRICT` stops implying "not revoked in the window". That weakens a registered
   306	    property, so it needs a signed amendment (A1 §A1.1).
   307	  - *P6 in TLA+.* `revoked` leaves the verifier's predicate and moves to an adjudicator layer.
   308	  - *Reporting.* `REV` is reported as "not evaluated by design" (R-5.33). The relying-party story says
   309	    so.
   310	  - *Other models.* The capstone and P9 are untouched.
   311	- **(B) The bundle carries post-anchor revocation status, LTV-style.**
   312	  - *A new statement.* Each channel signs "fp not revoked as of T" (or a revocation list), with T after
   313	    `anchor_time`, and a later anchor proves T.
   314	  - *Shared library.* It gains a statement form (a new signing domain in L-07 / D-1). The capstone and
   315	    first-link gain a status check, with a severing companion.
   316	  - *P6.* `revoked` gets a carrier and a freshness rule.
   317	  - *P4.* It defines the verdict when status is absent (`UNVERIFIABLE`, like `REVOKED_NO_POE`).
   318	  - *P8.* It encodes the statement.
   319	  - *Augmentation.* The status is added after shipping, outside the signed bytes, so it must be
   320	    self-authenticating. It is "late by construction", like the TLR, and needs a delivery path (G-51).
   321	  - *P9.* Its inputs stay inside the bundle.
   322	- **(C) A live or logged source.**
   323	  - *Conflicts.* It conflicts with R-5.10 ("no live DNS … no third-party log") and with A4.6 ("a log …
   324	    may never feed a verdict"). It breaks P9, because the verdict would depend on when the query is made.
   325	  - *What it would take.* Amending R-5.10 and P9's inspection criterion, and modelling the verdict as a
   326	    function of external state (P4, P9).
   327	  - *Variant.* Confining the source to the adjudicator turns C into A.
    65	
    66	**The honest ranking.** Two objectives, strictly ranked, and the ranking is
    67	the discipline:
    68	
    69	1. **Primary — the demonstration.** Close the operational-envelope gap (§7):
    70	   show that I can build, validate, observe, secure, and deploy an attestation
    71	   service to a beta-quality bar, with the engineering rigor visible in the
    72	   repository itself. This is the objective. It succeeds or fails on its own,
    73	   independent of whether anyone ever pays for the service.
    74	
    75	2. **Secondary — possible commercial service (upside, not premise).** Tessera
    76	   may become a viable attestation-as-a-service. If it does, good — but the
    77	   revenue-bearing pieces (billing/payment integration, PCI *certification*,
    78	   customer acquisition, scaling for real traffic) are **deferred until
    79	   validated paying demand exists**, and built no sooner. The product dream
    80	   does not expand the demonstration's scope. Note the distinction the primary
    81	   objective *does* honor: Tessera is architected **to the PCI standard**
    82	   (minimal cardholder-data environment, segmentation, key custody) as a matter
    83	   of engineering discipline — designing to the bar is demonstration; obtaining
    84	   the certificate is product, and waits.
    85	
    86	**Credibility line (non-negotiable):** every architecture decision here is
    87	the human author (Tony) in collaboration with AI agents. The purpose of
    88	the AI agents is to accelerate the typing, push back on my decisions, clarify
    89	them, but not to _make_ those decisions without consultation.
