    93	## 2. Hypotheses (falsifiable, dated)
    94	
    95	A key aspect of designing this project is to identify the anticipated
    96	criteria for evaluating the project itself.
    97	
    98	The work is decomposed into dependency-ordered bands. The bands matter because
    99	they have different risk profiles, and the hypotheses are stated per band rather
   100	than lumped into one figure. **Band 0 precedes all of them:**
   205	  backdate, break-chain, strip-one-signature — and each degradation scenario,
   206	  shows the verifier emits the **correct explicit verification state** (see
   207	  §4.6): `VALID_STRICT`, `VALID_DEGRADED(policy)`, `INVALID`, or `UNVERIFIABLE`.
   208	  Emitting the *right* state for every case is the criterion — including the
   209	  fail-open trap of returning `VALID` where the honest answer is `UNVERIFIABLE`.
   210	- **H1b (band 2 — vertical slice speed):** A local vertical slice (issue →
   211	  verify → chain a receipt, all on LocalStack) sustaining **1 attestation/second**
   212	  lands within **7 calendar days** of the Band 1 freeze. The 1/sec figure is a
   213	  *functional throughput* check (the pipeline sustains a steady rate without
   214	  deadlock or chain corruption), explicitly **not** a real-infrastructure
   215	  performance claim — LocalStack's performance characteristics are not AWS's.
   216	  *Clock (as a state machine, no ambiguity):*
   217	  H0 resolved → Phase 1a implements the cloud-independent crypto core →
   218	  **H1a freeze commit** (dated, signed; this both closes H1a and opens the H1b
   219	  window) → Phase 1b builds the LocalStack slice → **H1b evidence commit** must
   220	  land within 7 calendar days of the H1a freeze commit. Both endpoints live in
   221	  the anchored git history; elapsed time is auditable from the timestamps, not
   222	  asserted from memory.
   223	  *Falsified if:* no H1b evidence commit demonstrating 1/sec sustained on
   224	  LocalStack lands within 7 calendar days of the H1a freeze commit.
   225	- **H2 (division of labor):** The architecture decisions are demonstrably
   226	  mine; AI handled mechanics. *Falsified if:* the multi-model review panel (the
   227	  same ≥3 unrelated LLM models that adversarially review security in §5, here
   228	  tasked to interrogate architecture ownership) finds a load-bearing decision I
   229	  cannot justify without reconstructing it from the AI's reasoning. The panel is a
   230	  proxy for the only reviewer who matters: someone reading the repository to
   231	  see what was actually done.
   232	- **H3 (cost discipline):** Three separate numbers, so none can be trivially
   233	  contradicted by the pricing page:
   234	  - **demo-cycle variable cost under $25** — a complete cycle (`terraform apply`
   235	    → run the full red-bar + load suite → `terraform destroy`) at standard
   236	    on-demand pricing;
   237	  - **persistent monthly footprint under $5/month** — the intentional always-on
   238	    pieces (each customer-managed KMS key ~$1/mo, a Route 53 hosted zone
   239	    ~$0.50/mo, the ledger's S3 bucket) are low single-digit dollars, *not*
   240	    "cents";
   241	  - **idle compute exactly $0** — nothing *runs* between demonstrations (no
   242	    Fargate task, idle EC2, or provisioned capacity).
   243	
   244	  Cost is measured against *standard* pricing, not free-tier: free-tier credits
   245	  may cover actual spend, but H3 evidences cost *governance*, not subsidy.
   715	- a machine-checked formal model (TLA+ and/or Lean4) of the attestation
   716	  construction and its claimed invariants exists, and the proof — resting on
   717	  cited external hardness assumptions, proving *our own composition* — holds;
   718	- the agreement gate has passed (author and AI each independently judge the
   719	  model faithful to the claim);
   720	- the informal written proof (the author's defend-it-cold account) is in the
   721	  repository.
   722	
   723	The gate is **machine-checked**, by design. Independent *human* formal-methods
   724	review is *not* a resolution requirement under the no-spend constraint (H3) — it
   725	is an optional enhancement gated on a revenue stream, and its absence is a
   726	declared residual risk (no review, machine or human, guarantees a future party
   727	won't find a hole). This keeps the bar rigorous *and* achievable in our control,
   728	rather than set where it can never be met.
   729	
   730	Only then does **Phase 1a** begin (implementing the cloud-independent crypto
   731	core), marked by an explicit, dated, signed commit declaring the build runway
   732	open. The 7-day H1b window opens later, at the H1a freeze commit (§2).
   733	
   734	**If H0 cannot be met, the project does not proceed as designed — and that is a
   735	valid, honest outcome of this pre-registration, not a failure to conceal.** A
   736	highlight reel cannot say "we stopped because the foundation did not hold." A
   737	real pre-registration can, and that is precisely where its credibility comes
   738	from. The central guarantee of an attestation service is not something to
   739	assert and build around; it is something to prove, or to honestly report we
   740	could not. Building on an unproven foundation would be theater — the one
   741	outcome this project exists to refute.
# Tool decision: ProVerif (Tamarin rejected)

Committed per Amendment 1 §A1.4 **before any substantive proof work**.
Switching tools later requires a signed amendment naming the blocker.

## Decision

**ProVerif 2.05** is the symbolic protocol verifier for the Band 0
composition properties (P1–P3, P7 under the A1.3 adversary).

**Rejected alternative: Tamarin 1.12.0.**

## How the decision was made (division of labor, honestly)

The comparison ran the pre-registered spike (SPIKE.md): the same P2
anti-stripping fragment in both tools, correct and deliberately-broken
variants, judged on the three criteria fixed before either model was
written. The recommendation was formed by the AI collaborator (Claude)
on those criteria; the author (Tony Mason) read both correct models cold
and ratified the recommendation without override. Author's criterion-2
assessment, on the record: both models read as reasonable; this was his
first ProVerif model, and it was readable regardless.

## Scoring against the pre-registered criteria

1. **Expressiveness — tie.** Both models state the fragment in ~100
   lines with no contortions. Tamarin needed one `restriction` to pin
   single-issuer; ProVerif got it structurally. Neither hand-encoded
   anything unnatural.
2. **Author-readability — roughly a tie.** The author can read and
   contest both. Tamarin's multiset-rewrite rules sit closer to the TLA+
   state-machine idiom he already owns; ProVerif's applied pi calculus
   was new to him and still readable on first contact.
3. **Counterexample quality — edge to ProVerif, and the tie-breaker.**
   ProVerif's attack derivation is a numbered prose narrative, readable
   unaided in the committed `.out` evidence file
   (`proverif/p2_commitment_broken.out`). Tamarin's falsification is a
   constraint-solving proof script, opaque cold; its graphical trace
   explorer is good but requires running an interactive server. For a
   project whose evidence must speak from the repository, the
   self-explanatory counterexample wins.

Explicitly NOT criteria, per SPIKE.md: proof speed (both ~1–2s here),
whether the property verified (both tools verified the correct model and
falsified the broken one — no tool-shopping signal exists in this
comparison), and tool fashion.

## Context recorded, not counted

The author noted that recent TEE verification work also uses ProVerif —
reassuring ecosystem depth, but "who else uses it" is not one of the
three pre-registered criteria, so it is recorded here as context and was
not a deciding reason. (Flagged by the AI collaborator under A1.4's own
anti-tool-shopping discipline.)

## Consequences

- All symbolic lemmas for P1–P3/P7 are written in ProVerif, each with a
  prose mapping to its A1.2 property (A1.4 obligation).
- The cross-model correspondence mapping (A1.4) runs between the TLA+
  specs and the ProVerif models.
- Tamarin remains installed (`~/.local/bin/tamarin-prover`) and its
  spike artifacts remain committed as the comparison evidence; it is not
  used for proof work absent a signed amendment.
