     1	# Tool decision: ProVerif (Tamarin rejected)
     2	
     3	Committed per Amendment 1 §A1.4 **before any substantive proof work**.
     4	Switching tools later requires a signed amendment naming the blocker.
     5	
     6	## Decision
     7	
     8	**ProVerif 2.05** is the symbolic protocol verifier for the Band 0
     9	composition properties (P1–P3, P7 under the A1.3 adversary).
    10	
    11	**Rejected alternative: Tamarin 1.12.0.**
    12	
    13	## How the decision was made (division of labor, honestly)
    14	
    15	The comparison ran the pre-registered spike (SPIKE.md): the same P2
    16	anti-stripping fragment in both tools, correct and deliberately-broken
    17	variants, judged on the three criteria fixed before either model was
    18	written. The recommendation was formed by the AI collaborator (Claude)
    19	on those criteria; the author (Tony Mason) read both correct models cold
    20	and ratified the recommendation without override. Author's criterion-2
    21	assessment, on the record: both models read as reasonable; this was his
    22	first ProVerif model, and it was readable regardless.
    23	
    24	## Scoring against the pre-registered criteria
    25	
    26	1. **Expressiveness — tie.** Both models state the fragment in ~100
    27	   lines with no contortions. Tamarin needed one `restriction` to pin
    28	   single-issuer; ProVerif got it structurally. Neither hand-encoded
    29	   anything unnatural.
    30	2. **Author-readability — roughly a tie.** The author can read and
    31	   contest both. Tamarin's multiset-rewrite rules sit closer to the TLA+
    32	   state-machine idiom he already owns; ProVerif's applied pi calculus
    33	   was new to him and still readable on first contact.
    34	3. **Counterexample quality — edge to ProVerif, and the tie-breaker.**
    35	   ProVerif's attack derivation is a numbered prose narrative, readable
    36	   unaided in the committed `.out` evidence file
    37	   (`proverif/p2_commitment_broken.out`). Tamarin's falsification is a
    38	   constraint-solving proof script, opaque cold; its graphical trace
    39	   explorer is good but requires running an interactive server. For a
    40	   project whose evidence must speak from the repository, the
    41	   self-explanatory counterexample wins.
    42	
    43	Explicitly NOT criteria, per SPIKE.md: proof speed (both ~1–2s here),
    44	whether the property verified (both tools verified the correct model and
    45	falsified the broken one — no tool-shopping signal exists in this
    46	comparison), and tool fashion.
    47	
    48	## Context recorded, not counted
    49	
    50	The author noted that recent TEE verification work also uses ProVerif —
    51	reassuring ecosystem depth, but "who else uses it" is not one of the
    52	three pre-registered criteria, so it is recorded here as context and was
    53	not a deciding reason. (Flagged by the AI collaborator under A1.4's own
    54	anti-tool-shopping discipline.)
    55	
    56	## Consequences
    57	
    58	- All symbolic lemmas for P1–P3/P7 are written in ProVerif, each with a
    59	  prose mapping to its A1.2 property (A1.4 obligation).
    60	- The cross-model correspondence mapping (A1.4) runs between the TLA+
    61	  specs and the ProVerif models.
    62	- Tamarin remains installed (`~/.local/bin/tamarin-prover`) and its
    63	  spike artifacts remain committed as the comparison evidence; it is not
    64	  used for proof work absent a signed amendment.
